{# ============================================================================
   HYPER SHOP PRODUCTS — the grid injected into the shop page
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   ⚠️  Qumra mounts this widget into
       #products-grid-wrapper[data-qumra-section="shop-products"] and re-renders
       it on every filter / sort / page change. It runs INSIDE the
       collectionFilter() Alpine scope, so goToPage() is available here.

   ⚠️  Three contracts, all load-bearing:
     · .shop-products-content — style.css hides this markup unless it sits
       inside [data-qumra-section], so a stray server-rendered copy of the grid
       can never appear twice on the page.
     · #products-count-data — main.js copies its innerHTML into the toolbar
       count after every run. Keep the id, keep it hidden.
     · the cards come from ui/product-card.njk, the same partial the home page
       uses, so a filtered grid matches the row that linked to it.
   ========================================================================= #}
<div class="shop-products-content">

{% set d = widget.data %}
{# /shop and /shop/<handle> send context.shop; /collections/<handle> sends
   context.products + context.pagination. #}
{% set products = context.shop.products or context.products or [] %}
{% set pagination = context.shop.pagination or context.pagination %}
{% set totalItems = pagination.totalItems if pagination else (products | length) %}
{# No ratio setting here on purpose. `shop-page` owns it and pushes it down as
   `--hy-card-ratio` on the section, which reaches these cards AND the ones
   main.js rebuilds after a filter. A second copy on this widget is what made
   the setting look dead: the two disagreed and each won on a different path. #}
{% set ratio = '' %}

{# ---- count source ----
   main.js mirrors this into the toolbar after every filter run. It does NOT
   run on first paint — this partial is server rendered — so the small script
   below does that one initial copy. Without it the count sits empty until the
   shopper touches a filter. #}
<div id="products-count-data" style="display: none;">
  {% ui "count.njk", n=(totalItems or 0), kind='product' %}
</div>

{% if (products | length) > 0 %}
  <div class="hy-shop-grid hx-shop-grid">
    {% for p in products %}
      {% ui "hx-card.njk", product=p, sale_badge=t('featured.sale_badge'), low_badge=t('featured.low_badge'), note='', rating='on', thumbs='on', stock='on', quick='on', atc='on', hover='on' %}
    {% endfor %}
  </div>

  {# ---- pagination — goToPage() comes from the collectionFilter scope ---- #}
  {% if pagination and pagination.totalPages > 1 %}
    <nav class="hy-pager" aria-label="pagination">
      <button type="button" class="hy-pager-btn"
              {% if pagination.currentPage <= 1 %}disabled{% endif %}
              @click="goToPage({{ pagination.currentPage - 1 }})"
              aria-label="{{t('common.prev')}}">
        <svg class="rtl-flip" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" aria-hidden="true">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M15 6 9 12l6 6"/>
        </svg>
      </button>

      {% for n in range(1, pagination.totalPages + 1) %}
        {# Show the first, the last, and a window around the current page —
           a 40-page collection would otherwise print 40 buttons. #}
        {% if n == 1 or n == pagination.totalPages or (n >= pagination.currentPage - 1 and n <= pagination.currentPage + 1) %}
          <button type="button" class="hy-pager-btn {{ 'is-active' if n == pagination.currentPage }}"
                  @click="goToPage({{ n }})">{{ n }}</button>
        {% elif n == pagination.currentPage - 2 or n == pagination.currentPage + 2 %}
          <span class="hy-pager-gap">…</span>
        {% endif %}
      {% endfor %}

      <button type="button" class="hy-pager-btn"
              {% if pagination.currentPage >= pagination.totalPages %}disabled{% endif %}
              @click="goToPage({{ pagination.currentPage + 1 }})"
              aria-label="{{t('common.next')}}">
        <svg class="rtl-flip" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" aria-hidden="true">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="m9 6 6 6-6 6"/>
        </svg>
      </button>
    </nav>
  {% endif %}
{% else %}
  {# THREE different kinds of "nothing", not two.

     · A filter or a search that matched nothing is a real answer, and gets one.
     · An EMPTY COLLECTION is also a real answer — someone opened a category the
       merchant has not stocked. This case used to fall through to the branch
       below, so a shopper on a live store was shown eight fake product cards
       and the line "Sample content — pick your products in the Theme Editor",
       which is a sentence written for the merchant and addressed to a customer.
       `hasFilters` alone could not see it: an empty collection carries no
       applied filter and no query.
     · A store with NO products at all is a merchant looking at their own
       unfinished shop, and a grid of placeholders tells them more than an
       apology does. That is the only case the skeleton is for.

     The way out matters as much as the message: a dead end with no link is how
     a session ends. Both real-answer cases get a route back to the full shop. #}
  {% set col = context.shop.collection if (context.shop and context.shop.collection and context.shop.collection._id) else (context.collection | default(null)) %}
  {# ⚠️  What the server actually sends (measured on the dev store):
       context.shop.filters.applied = { filters: { price: {min}, 101: [..] }, sort, q }
     `context.shop.appliedFilters` does not exist, so this test was always
     false — a price range that matched nothing fell through to the merchant
     placeholder grid: eight skeleton cards and "0 products" above them, on a
     live shop, for a customer. `| length` counts an object's keys here. #}
  {% set applied = context.shop.filters.applied if (context.shop and context.shop.filters and context.shop.filters.applied) else {} %}
  {% set hasFilters = (applied.filters | default({}, true) | length) > 0
                      or (applied.q | default('', true) | length) > 0
                      or (context.query.q | default('', true) | length) > 0
                      or (context.shop.appliedFilters | default([], true) | length) > 0
                      or (context.shop.query | default('', true) | length) > 0 %}
  {% if hasFilters or col %}
    <div class="hy-empty">
      <span class="hy-empty-title">
        {{ t('pages.search.no_results') if hasFilters else t('products.no_products') }}
      </span>
      {% if hasFilters %}
        <p class="m-0 text-sm">{{t('pages.search.try_different')}}</p>
      {% endif %}
      <a href="/shop" class="hy-btn hy-btn--outline hy-btn--sm" style="margin-block-start: var(--space-4);">
        {{t('pages.shop.all_products')}}
      </a>
    </div>
  {% else %}
    <div class="hy-shop-grid hx-shop-grid">
      {% ui "placeholder.njk", kind='product', count='8', image_ratio=ratio %}
    </div>
    <p class="hy-ph-note">{{ t('common.placeholder_note') }}</p>
  {% endif %}
{% endif %}

<script>
  (function () {
    /* ---- 1. put the grid where it belongs -------------------------------
       Qumra renders this widget in PAGE ORDER (it is the second entry in
       pages/shop.json) and, on a filter run, again inside
       #products-grid-wrapper. style.css only shows the copy that sits inside
       that wrapper — otherwise the grid would appear twice.

       On a first load the mounted copy does not exist yet, so the only copy
       present is the page-order one and the rule hides it: the shopper lands
       on an empty results column with a working product count beside it, and
       the products appear only after they touch a filter.

       Moving the page-order copy into the wrapper fixes that at the source. It
       is a no-op when Qumra did mount one (`contains` is already true), so it
       is safe whichever way the platform renders. ------------------------- */
    var wrap = document.getElementById('products-grid-wrapper');
    var content = document.currentScript && document.currentScript.closest('.shop-products-content');
    if (wrap && content && !wrap.contains(content)) wrap.appendChild(content);

    /* ---- 2. one initial copy of the count into the toolbar --------------
       main.js owns every later update; it does not run on first paint
       because this partial is server rendered. */
    var src = document.getElementById('products-count-data');
    var dst = document.getElementById('products-count-toolbar');
    if (src && dst) dst.innerHTML = src.innerHTML;
  })();
</script>

</div>
